<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geng Jingzhong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1682">1682</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geng Jingzhong was a Chinese general who inherited his family's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories">Three Feudatories</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. He joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a>'s rebellion in 1674 but surrendered to the Qing in 1676 when his position collapsed. After the revolt ended he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and executed in 1682.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geng-jingzhong.jpg" alt="Geng Jingzhong" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author in Qing Dynasty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geng_Jingzhong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Geng Jingzhong, written Keng Ching-chung in Wade–Giles, was a powerful military commander of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. He inherited the title of Prince of Jingnan from his father, Geng Jimao, who had inherited it from Jingzhong's grandfather, Geng Zhongming. His father had both of his sons made court attendants under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a> and married to women of the imperial Aisin Gioro clan, which brought them the rank of efu given to the husbands of Qing princesses. Geng Jingzhong married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooge">Hooge</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>, and his brother Geng Zhaozhong a granddaughter of Prince Abatai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Geng was firmly entrenched as a quasi-independent ruler of his principality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. In 1674 he rebelled against Qing rule together with the other two of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories">Three Feudatories</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-zhixin">Shang Zhixin</a>, who also governed enormous principalities in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Qing armies, including forces commanded by Jieshu that took Yanping, eventually defeated him.</p><p>After his surrender in 1676 Geng pledged to help put down the other rebellious feudatories, and the Qing used his troops against them until the civil war ended. Soon after the final Qing victory in 1681, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> had him executed for treason by slow slicing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Another brother, Geng Juzhong, who had married Princess Heshou Roujia, a daughter of Prince Yolo, Prince An, was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> at the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> throughout the rebellion. He was not punished for his brother's revolt, held the rank of a viscount of the third class, and died of natural causes in 1687.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Geng Jingzhong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With <b>Geng Jingzhong</b> in Fujian and Shang Zhixin in Guangdong, Wu at first controlled most of southern China, but his claim to restore Chinese rule was undermined by his own past.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhou-wu-sangui">Zhou (Wu Sangui)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1672" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Geng Jingzhong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1672">What was here in 1672? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
